{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when his master heard</b> — at the time of intercourse she said to him, \"Your slave did to me things such as these\" — matters of intercourse such as these. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 39:19). And thus they said (Genesis Rabbah 87:9): Rabbi Abbahu said, at the time of intercourse.\n\nAnd I am astonished, for Joseph's master was a eunuch, and he married his wife in his youth, and they expounded (ibid. 86:3), \"the eunuch of Pharaoh\" — this teaches that he acquired him only for intercourse and the Holy One, blessed be He, castrated him in his body. And furthermore, how could she defile herself and make herself repugnant in her husband's eyes by telling him that she committed adultery, whether under duress or willingly — she deserved to be killed, for why did she not cry out at the outset so that he would flee, as she did in the end? Now, to the men of her household she said, \"He came to me to lie with me\" — not that he lay with her, but that he came to do so and I cried out and he fled, and all the more so would she conceal the matter from her husband. And if you say she did it so that his anger would flare and he would kill him — indeed, a slave who attempts to violate the wife of his master, the prince, is liable to death.\n\nIt is possible that they wished to interpret \"such as these things\" as matters of intimacy — uncovering of nakedness and manual contact — not actual intercourse, for his master was a eunuch in his body, having a disease that removed all desire, like the condition of the שחוף. But according to the plain sense there is no need for this, for the כ\"ף is not for comparison to something else; its meaning is \"these things,\" and like it (Genesis 24:28), \"And she told her mother's house such things as these,\" and likewise, \"And when he spoke with me such words as these, I set my face toward the ground\" (Daniel 10:15), and many similar examples. Or one may say: when his master heard his wife's words — that she said to him, \"Your slave did to me such things as these that I reported immediately to the men of the house\" — his anger flared. And it is possible that the use of the כ\"ף here serves for emphasis, as in (Genesis 44:7), \"Why does my lord speak such words as these,\" and \"They have called me such as these\" (Leviticus 10:19).\n\nNow, because of his master's love for Joseph he did not kill him — or it was a miracle from HaShem, or he had doubt about her words since he knew in his heart the righteousness of Joseph. And thus they said (Genesis Rabbah 87:9): he said to him, \"I know that this is not from you, but only so as not to mix refuse into my sons.\""], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 39, :notes {0 "The word שחוף (shechuf) appears in rabbinic medical terminology denoting a wasting disease; rendered here hyper-literally as 'the שחוף' to preserve Ramban's technical usage. The Aramaic quotation from Genesis Rabbah at the close is translated into idiomatic English."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:42:19.994153122Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}